--TEST--
Core\Mail::send reaches only an endpoint an operator named, so an unconfigured one is refused — ADR 0082 § 2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The whole of ADR 0082 § 2's transport rule, from the outside: `$endpoint`
// selects a `[mail.<name>]` block in root-owned `nvs.toml` under the `mail.send`
// capability, and there is no host, port or credential parameter anywhere on
// the row. So a program running under a deployment that named no such block
// cannot send at all — there is nothing for it to fall back to, and no
// argument through which it could supply one.
try {
    Core\Mail::send(
        "transactional",
        ["user@example.com"],
        "Your receipt",
        "Thank you.",
    );
    echo "sent", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo "refused", "\n";
}

// Naming a different block does not change the answer, which is the "and no
// other" half: what a program writes here is a key into the operator's file,
// never a destination of its own.
try {
    Core\Mail::send("bulk", ["user@example.com"], "Your receipt", "Thank you.");
    echo "sent", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo "refused", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
refused
refused
